   "session_id": "20261016-173008-8dc4",
   "turns": [
    {
     "user": "Is my 3DCP mortar (w/b 0.30, s/b 1.5, 90 % OPC + 10 % silica fume, 0.6 % PCE, 0.2 % cellulose VMA) buildable for a 400 mm high, 1 m long single-filament wall printed with a 30 mm nozzle at 40 mm/s?",
     "text": "I’ll run the buildability check with the supplied mix and print geometry, then report the physical failure checks and any assumptions the calculator needs.\nThe calculator flags plastic collapse before the wall reaches the target height, and its free wall buckling check also fails. I’m checking the closest published print runs to put the model result in context.\n**Verdict: non-printable for the proposed wall.** The calculator predicts plastic collapse around layer 4 (about 59 mm high), well short of 400 mm. Its separate free-wall buckling check fails around layer 6. The empirical collapse estimate is 0.925, but the physical checks drive the verdict.\n\nThe check assumed a 15 mm layer height, 35.53 mm layer width, no dwell, and a 25 s cycle for each 1 m layer at 40 mm/s. It used the calculator’s default density of 2,100 kg/m³, stiffness ratio \\(E/\\tau_s=25\\), and safety factor of 1.5. The mix inputs were treated as product dosages by binder mass. No curing age or specimen default was needed for this fresh-state check.\n\nThe predicted initial static yield stress is **645 [28, 6,568] Pa** and the structuration rate is **0.586 [0.024, 14.518] Pa/s** (q50 [q10, q90]). The structuration model is weak, so these are order-of-magnitude guidance. A [published mortar analogue](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.conbuildmat.2025.143293) with the same OPC/silica-fume split and admixture dosages measured 578 Pa and 0.488 Pa/s; it also differed in water and sand ratios and contained fibres.\n\nAt the current cycle time, the calculator estimates that preventing *plastic collapse* would require either about **6,830 Pa initial static yield stress** or **9.75 Pa/s structuration**, with the other predicted property held fixed. Slowing the layer cycle to at least **416 s** is another calculated route for the plastic check, but it does not resolve the free-wall buckling result; the required print duration also needs an open-time check. Practical trials should therefore measure fresh yield stress and structuration, and test bracing or additional wall filaments alongside changes such as an accelerator, VMA adjustment, or lower w/b. The final mix needs a physical print trial.\n\nFiles: [assessment](<C:/Users/User/3D Printing Concrete Prediction/artifacts/reports/agent/20261016-173008-8dc4/assessment_job.md>), [cycle-time plot](<C:/Users/User/3D Printing Concrete Prediction/artifacts/reports/agent/20261016-173008-8dc4/cycle_sweep_job.png>), [cycle data](<C:/Users/User/3D Printing Concrete Prediction/artifacts/reports/agent/20261016-173008-8dc4/cycle_sweep_job.csv>), [literature analogues](<C:/Users/User/3D Printing Concrete Prediction/artifacts/reports/agent/20261016-173008-8dc4/literature.csv>).\n",
